import{_ as t,P as n,d as i,e as o,y as r,q as s,f as l}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.16 Organizations, Work, Leadership, and Labor Protection","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.16 Organizations, Work, Leadership, and Labor Protection","xkChars":0,"xkWords":578,"xkLessons":{"D.16":{"title":"D.16 Organizations, Work, Leadership, and Labor Protection","lead":"Restored output after replacing a person in charge may be a real improvement. If it still relies on a few people continually working overtime to fill gaps, sustainability and distribution remain to be assessed. Organizational research must return to the actual work chain; eligibility for adoption under a research framework must not become a threshold for workers' organizing and appeals.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4857–4876; source anchor section-d-16; short quotation, translated: 'Organizational survival, work capability, procedural improvement, and workers' lives are treated as separate outcomes; an established result in one does not vouch for the others.' The source passage for this topic is also preserved verbatim in source-slice.md; the explanation is separate from the source.","Organizational formation, growth, differentiation, exploration and exploitation, resource dependence, institutionalization, and exit require connections among tasks, knowledge, resources, authority, and actual work chains. Life-cycle models may suggest the kinds of conditional problems often encountered at a stage; they should not place every organization in an obligatory sequence. Legal registration, work networks, knowledge transmission, and service provision may also begin or end at different times. A single formation or closure date cannot stand in for them all.","Organizational learning requires distinguishing at least the formation of a scheme, leaving a record, actual invocation, lasting change, and outcomes. A training session may expose members to knowledge, and a policy may preserve a scheme, but later use in tasks, lasting changes to practice, and improved outcomes each require separate examination. Conversely, an unfinished specific test for a feedback-mediated learning branch does not cancel a general learning process already traced by available materials.","Isomorphic rules may provide coordination and protection, while decoupling may occur at the levels of text, implementation, or outcomes. Similar wording in documents does not establish identical implementation; identical implementation may also produce different outcomes because resources and environments differ. Decoupling sometimes concerns constraints, buffering, and local adaptation, and cannot uniformly be treated as deception. If false promotion or concealment actually occurs, it must be identified with corresponding materials.","Professional and informal networks may fill gaps in formal processes, but may also make access difficult for people without connections. Evaluation should record both the results of filling gaps and the conditions of exclusion. Helping some people does not negate others' barriers, while informality alone does not automatically make everything ineffective. What matters is how knowledge and opportunities circulate, and who has usable paths and channels for appeal.","Leadership effects should be located in agendas, resources, interpretation, authorization, and error correction. Attributing every success and failure to one person's temperament overlooks team skills, budgets, tools, and the division of labor. A shock after a person in charge leaves can identify a specific dependence under existing organizational conditions. This differs from the entire historical counterfactual in which that person never existed; one departure cannot directly identify the organization's entire origin.","Labor evaluation cannot consider output alone. Hours, pace, skills, income, health, hidden maintenance, and care may each change: increased output per unit may simultaneously depend on a few people's overload and unrecorded maintenance work. Examine who bears benefits and costs, whether the arrangement can continue, and whether substitutes and handover are available, rather than treating output maximization as the whole of work quality.","When comparing working-time materials, distinguish four kinds of information: statutory maxima, overtime-pay thresholds, schedules, and actual hours worked. Check the covered populations, time units, phased implementation, and exceptions. A reference to 40 hours in one place does not make it a universal maximum working week. A payment trigger under a particular regime differs from a person's actual workload, and the existence of a legal text does not establish actual payment or compliance. Specific legal questions require professional verification against the applicable jurisdiction and facts.","Workers' joint decision-making must be specified by subject matter and authority: raising issues, obtaining information, being consulted, bargaining, joint consent, dispute adjudication, and autonomous rule-making within an organization are different. Being consulted need not entail a veto, and an ability to bargain does not mean that every matter requires joint consent. Workers can establish their own mutual-aid and work rules; whether they can change an employer's or platform's rules requires separate examination of the particular institutions and authorization.","Members, owners, employees, and represented people may not coincide. A vote cannot replace checks of nomination, accountability, the scope of representation, and internal dissent; it does not establish that all affected workers participated or were covered. Research should distinguish who holds which eligibility, rather than bypassing scope and power with vague statements such as 'everyone voted.'","Exit, separation, merger, and collective bargaining involve contracts, knowledge, services, representational scope, implementation, and protection against retaliation. Their completion or success cannot be judged solely by the legal entity's name. Xikari's research eligibility constrains only the corresponding analytical assertions; it must not become an entry condition for workers' association, organizing, or appeals. People need not pass a theoretical diagnosis before their ability to raise their own claims is recognized.","Organizational-memory failures can occur in retention, retrieval, awareness, handling, or consequences. If old information was retrieved and read by the person in charge but did not enter handling, continuing to call it memory loss obscures the actual blockage. Improvement must address handling authority and incentives, rather than merely build another database. The nine entry points, in order, are life cycles in Greiner and Adizes; learning in March, Argyris, and Schön; resources and institutionalization in Pfeffer, Salancik, and Selznick; isomorphism and decoupling in Meyer, Rowan, DiMaggio, and Powell; bureaucracy, professions, and networks; leadership, entrepreneurship, and collective formation; the labor process and burnout; exit, merger, and memory; and workers' organizing, bargaining, and protection. The strongest objection worries that separating outcomes will deny improved output. Real output achievements should be retained while not allowing them to settle other outcomes."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Training or recording in a document","leftText":"A scheme is encountered or retained.","rightTitle":"Sustained invocation and improvement","rightText":"Later task use and changes to practice and outcomes require separate checking."},{"leftTitle":"Information was not retained or cannot be found","leftText":"A failure early in the memory chain.","rightTitle":"Known but not handled","rightText":"The blockage concerns handling and authority; it should not continue to be called memory loss indiscriminately."},{"leftTitle":"Overtime-pay threshold","leftText":"Reaching a specified number of hours triggers particular compensation.","rightTitle":"Statutory maximum and actual hours worked","rightText":"The maximum permitted and the actual amount of work are two other questions."},{"leftTitle":"Consultation and bargaining","leftText":"An ability to express views or negotiate.","rightTitle":"Joint consent and adjudication","rightText":"Actual decision-making authority is more specific; the word 'participation' cannot fill it in."},{"leftTitle":"Effect of the person in charge leaving","leftText":"A shock to a specific dependence within the existing organization.","rightTitle":"A world in which this person never existed","rightText":"Involves a different historical counterfactual that departure does not directly identify."}],"boundaries":["Do not treat organizational life cycles as a ladder of maturity every organization must climb.","An unfinished specific learning contract does not cancel traceable learning in general; training and documents also do not automatically establish effects.","Workers' organizing and appeals do not require entry through Xikari analytical eligibility; research does not replace their autonomous rights.","The working-time case explains conceptual distinctions in official materials, not legal compliance in an individual case. Rules and exceptions in other jurisdictions require separate verification.","All institutional and numerical teaching examples are hypothetical; they do not infer actual employees' health, motives, or employment relations."],"questions":["Why can the termination of a service not be judged solely from a legal entity's closure date?","Which items are most commonly conflated in working-time comparisons?","Where should a memory failure involving known but unhandled information be investigated?","Can improved output coexist with worsening working conditions?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-16/figures/section-d-16.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual official explanation: a 40-hour pay threshold differs from a limit on hours","href":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/23-flsa-overtime-pay"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual official explanation: a 40-hour pay threshold differs from a limit on hours","text":"U.S. Department of Labor Fact Sheet23, marked revised October 2019 on the page, links work beyond 40 hours per workweek to overtime pay for covered, nonexempt employees under the FLSA in the first Requirements paragraph. It separately states that the Act itself imposes no limit on weekly hours for employees aged 16 and over. The next paragraph defines a 168-hour workweek and prohibits averaging across weeks. Thus 40 is not a number that can simply be copied as a universal limit on working hours.","sourceLabel":"U.S. Department of Labor, Fact Sheet23; access verified 2026-10-14","sourceUrl":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/23-flsa-overtime-pay","sourceLocator":"Revised October 2019; first two Requirements paragraphs (lines 103–104); concluding statement of its general-information status (lines 123–124)","boundary":"The official general explanation as accessed is used only to distinguish concepts, not to declare anyone's schedule lawful. State law, other laws, industry restrictions, exemptions, and actual circumstances require separate verification. The material itself states that it is not a regulation with legal force.","href":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/23-flsa-overtime-pay"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.16","start_line":4948,"end_line":4967,"startLine":4948,"endLine":4967}],"outputId":"section-d-16","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Connect organizational stages to specific tasks, knowledge, resources, and authority, and distinguish the clocks of legal entities, networks, knowledge, and services.","Examine learning and memory link by link: formation, retention, retrieval, awareness, invocation, handling, lasting change, and outcomes.","Trace the actual channels of leadership and informal networks, considering both gap-filling and exclusion.","Evaluate working conditions separately. Working-time comparisons must distinguish maxima, pay thresholds, schedules, actual hours, and scope of application.","Check participation authority, representational scope, and protection against retaliation for each matter; evaluate organizational survival alongside workers' lives."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Developing the source's teaching example: restored output that still depends on overtime","text":"Suppose replacing the person in charge brings completed weekly tasks back from 80 to 100, but 20 still depend on two members continually doing additional work to fill gaps. Restored output can be recorded. Sustainability, the distribution of overtime costs and compensation, and whether work can be taken over if someone leaves remain unresolved.","assumptions":"Task counts and members are teaching assumptions, not actual labor data.","boundary":"Additional work is not automatically classified as voluntary or unlawful; specific facts and applicable norms are needed."},{"title":"Teaching construction: known but unhandled is not the same as unfindable","text":"Suppose an accident warning is stored in a database. The person in charge retrieved and read it and mentioned it in a meeting, but lacked authority to move resources, and the necessary action was not taken afterward. Retention, retrieval, and awareness can all hold, while the handling-chain blockage still needs explanation. Building another identical database may not address the problem.","assumptions":"A fictional safety warning; no actual safety decision is provided.","boundary":"Actual causes require materials. The example only distinguishes links and does not prejudge personal fault."},{"title":"Teaching construction: consultation does not entail joint consent","text":"Suppose members can raise scheduling issues and receive replies, but managers still decide the final schedule alone. The consultation channel can be recognized, but members cannot be said to have obtained a right of joint consent on every schedule. If workers also have autonomous mutual-aid rules, their internal effect must be distinguished from authority to change the employer's rules.","assumptions":"A hypothetical organization; no actual employment contract is interpreted.","boundary":"Limited authority does not negate consultation's value, and one vote cannot fill in representational authorization."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: the same average hides concentrated burdens","text":"Four people's additional working hours are (0,0,10,10), averaging 5 hours. Another arrangement is (5,5,5,5), also averaging 5 hours. Total additional hours are 20 in both, but their concentration differs. The average alone does not establish identical distribution, much less reveal who is willing to bear the hours.","assumptions":"The hours are constructed; they do not correspond to statutory thresholds or actual health risks.","boundary":"Fairness still depends on roles, choices, and burden conditions; equal distribution does not automatically decide it."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The new policy has been written into a document, so everyone has learned it and tasks have improved over the long term.","effect":"Retention, actual invocation, lasting change, and effects each require materials.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"The person in charge read the old report but did not handle it, so this is simply organizational memory loss.","effect":"Known materials failed to enter handling; investigate authority and organizational blockages rather than retention alone.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Average overtime is the same, so burden distribution and sustainability are the same.","effect":"Distributions and choice conditions may differ; an average is insufficient.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why can the termination of a service not be judged solely from a legal entity's closure date?","answer":"Networks, knowledge, and services may continue through handover, or may already have been interrupted while the legal entity remains in existence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Which items are most commonly conflated in working-time comparisons?","answer":"Statutory maxima, overtime-pay thresholds, planned schedules, and actual working hours, with populations, units, and exceptions also checked.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Where should a memory failure involving known but unhandled information be investigated?","answer":"Handling channels, resources, authority, incentives, and consequences, rather than repeatedly assuming there was no storage.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can improved output coexist with worsening working conditions?","answer":"Yes. Record achievements and the distribution of costs separately, without either erasing the other.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-16/figures/section-d-16.png","alt":"Legal entities, work networks, knowledge, and services may run on different clocks. Learning extends from formation to outcomes; recording ≠ invocation and improvement, while learning in general may still hold. Locate memory failures link by link: retention, retrieval, awareness, handling, and consequences. Hours, pace, skills, income, health, maintenance, and care are distinct; output does not encompass quality. Distinguish maxima, pay thresholds, schedules, and actual hours; separately check populations, units, and exceptions. Specify participation authority; distinguish members, owners, and employees; impose no theoretical entry threshold on organizing and appeals. Drawn by the lesson author from D.16; organizational and working-time examples are fictional, and the official explanation does not replace legal verification of an individual case.","caption":"Drawn by the lesson author from D.16; organizational and working-time examples are fictional, and the official explanation does not replace legal verification of an individual case."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a proposal for later design. Separately switching the failure location in the memory chain, participation authority, and the distribution of work burdens would test which conclusions should be retained and which suspended.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original official explanation directly distinguishes a pay threshold from the Act's own provisions on hours. It is used to prevent copying numbers incorrectly across jurisdictions, not to provide legal advice for an individual case."},"openQuestions":["The complete original research for 16.01–16.09 was not obtained; no actual organization or labor dispute was examined.","The Department of Labor case checks only the specified general explanation. It does not complete an investigation of individual applicability, state law, industry exceptions, or actual compliance."],"taskId":"S-D-16","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research draft, full-text lines 4769–4788; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["D.16: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"dol-fs23","title":"Fact Sheet23: Overtime Pay Requirements of the FLSA","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/23-flsa-overtime-pay","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Revised October 2019; accessed 2026-10-14","locator":"Revision date; first two Requirements paragraphs, lines 103–104; concluding statement, lines 123–124","claim_support":["40 hours as the overtime-pay threshold for the specified covered, nonexempt population; the Act's own statement on weekly hours for those aged 16 and over; the 168-hour week and prohibition on averaging across weeks"],"limits":["Only an official general explanation, not the complete regulations or advice on an individual case. Other jurisdictions, industries, exceptions, and actual compliance were not examined."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.16 Organizations, Work, Leadership, and Labor Protection; Chinese source lines 4857–4876; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-16/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-16/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-16/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-16/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"e40bfb0e1799c5ecf35e53a1b55f8ccb7152f2a51fa4220c193aa0a644fa43b0","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-16.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-16.md"}`),c={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-16.md"};function d(u,e,h,p,m,g){const a=n("SectionLesson");return i(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r("",1)),s(a,{section:"D.16"}),e[1]||(e[1]=l("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=t(c,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
